  "prompt_text": "The figure links coastal “blue carbon” ecosystems to carbon storage and to the policy history around them. The upper schematic shows a land-to-sea shoreline transitioning through tidal marshes, mangroves, and seagrass, with nearby kelp and upland vegetation. Blue spheres represent carbon moving from land and water into coastal sediments and plant biomass. Arrows indicate CO₂ exchange with the atmosphere and strong downward burial into soils beneath marshes, mangroves, and seagrass meadows, emphasizing that these habitats trap and store carbon over time, including below ground and below the high-water line.\n\nThe lower panel places this science in a timeline from the late 1970s to 2020. It mixes major climate-policy milestones with key blue-carbon science and management steps. Early climate events such as the first World Climate Conference, creation of the IPCC, the UN climate convention, and the Kyoto Protocol are followed by blue-carbon-specific developments, including recognition of marine macrophytes as major carbon sinks, the coining of the term “blue carbon,” and publication of policy and science blueprints. More recent entries highlight wetland accounting methods, standards for restoration, and project guidance, while also noting that destruction of blue carbon ecosystems can contribute substantially to global emissions.",
